[
    {
        "content": "<p>Suppose that I have a syntax category that might match one of two alternatives:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">declare_syntax_cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"cat\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">declare_syntax_cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">dog</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"dog\"</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">dog</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">declare_syntax_cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;|&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">dog</span><span class=\"o\">):</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>If I have a piece of syntax that matched <code>cat_or_dog</code>, how can I test which it matched?</p>\n<p>I tried this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ha</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Lean</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">TSyntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`cat_or_dog</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">dog</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">panic!</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"no parse\"</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">macro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"foo\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ha</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">pure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Lean</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Syntax</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mkNatLit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I was surprised when the compiler reported \"redundant alternative <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/2\">#2</a>\" on my line that attempts to match the <code>_dog</code> category above.  Indeed, when I run</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">dog</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>the output is</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>showing that it matched the first case.</p>\n<p>If I add a line <code>dbg_trace (repr c)</code> to the macro above to print out the detailed syntax tree, I can see that it does record that it matched a dog, not a cat: there is a node in there with kind <code>_dogDog</code>.   So the information is there.  But practically speaking, how can I tell whether I have a dog or a cat?  :)</p>",
        "id": 615489989,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1786301411
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is a bad idea, do</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">declare_syntax_cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"cat\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">declare_syntax_cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">dog</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"dog\"</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">dog</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">declare_syntax_cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">dog</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>instead</p>",
        "id": 615505468,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1786314601
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>An <code>&lt;|&gt;</code> is not tagged, that is, it only knows the syntax inside and not which branch it comes from. If you <em>really</em> wanted you could probably check which syntax category a syntax is from by looking at the parser name but that's not very nice to work with.</p>",
        "id": 615505629,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1786314754
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Aha - I had no idea that using &lt;|&gt; was any different than having two separate <code>syntax</code> declarations, one for each branch.  I can confirm that with the separate declarations everything works fine.  This is very helpful, thanks!</p>",
        "id": 615506913,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1786316199
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>By the way I've realized there's another important different between using &lt;|&gt; and having separate declarations for two alternatives.   With &lt;|&gt; it will not backtrack after any token matches (as described in the language reference), however with separate declarations the parser will fully backtrack.</p>\n<p>For example, if I write</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">declare_syntax_cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"big\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"black\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"cat\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">declare_syntax_cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">dog</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"big\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"black\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"dog\"</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">dog</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">declare_syntax_cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;|&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">dog</span><span class=\"o\">):</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>then <code>cat_or_dog</code> will only match \"big black cat\", since the parser won't backtrack after <code> _cat</code> matches \"big\".</p>\n<p>However, if I change the declaration of <code>cat_or_dog</code> to</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">declare_syntax_cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">dog</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat_or_dog</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>then it will match either \"big black cat\" or \"big black dog\".</p>",
        "id": 615572385,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1786350841
    }
]